==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Crazed Bahamut Cat''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Awakened Bahamut Cat''' at level 20 after completing [[Into the Future]] Chapter 3.

==Strategy/Usage==
In his first two forms, Bahamut would serve as your main backliner at the start of the game, carrying average cost, range, recharge and workable DPS. This role is comparable to various [[:Category:Uber Rare Cats|Uber Rare]]s, providing consistent damage output that is crucial for almost all lineups.

Bahamut's main points of interest are his range and solid damage output. Threats like [[Le'boin]] and [[Dagshund]] are much easier to counter with his presence, since he can eradicate them in one or two hits with a massive 85k damage nuke every 20 seconds at level 30. Crowds aren't safe either, as his area attack covers his whole range.

However, later in the game, these two forms of Bahamut Cat are generally overshadowed by [[Ururun Wolf (Special Cat)|Ururun Wolf]], who costs slightly more but has a much more consistent damage output due to faster attacks and short foreswing, which also reduces reliance on support, and a useful Knockback ability, to say nothing of the various Uber Rare backliners the player may have. However, Bahamut Cat is still a good option alongside Ururun Wolf for additional backline DPS even up until the player unlocks [[Urs & Fenrir (Special Cat)|Urs & Fenrir]], and even then, offers some interesting advantages.

This style of attack is what makes him inconvenient to use, however. Bahamut Cat suffers from a limiting 4-second foreswing. This causes him to miss attacks constantly, and missing an attack is very costly due to his long time between attacks. This foreswing also makes him unresponsive to aggressive pushers like [[Bore]] which may juggle if not kill him outright when he is exposed, and severely impacts his versatility into threats that outrange him, like [[Master A.]] and [[THE SLOTH]]. These issues are compounded by his meagre 25k HP, giving the player a low margin for error when using him.

However, having such a big burst of damage has advantages. It is very helpful when one needs to knock out a frail but threatening enemy, such as a buffed [[Celeboodle]] quickly, where a faster-attacking backliner might paradoxically take longer to kill it. His long foreswing, along with his standard range, also lets the player easily bait enemies into his range, which could be appealing to maximise the value from his sluggish attacks. Late into the game, Aku enemies start appearing and some of them have shields that regenerate when knocked back. Bahamut can have niche usage against them, as his high damage per hit allows him to skip several knockbacks from Aku Enemies, reducing the total shield health they may recover. For example, if Bahamut Cat somehow manages to land a hit on a 100% [[Midnite D.]] with a broken shield, he will skip 5 knockbacks or 200k hitpoints worth of shields. In situations where Bahamut is constantly moving, he will also be more efficient as he would spend time recharge his attack while moving, wasting less DPS than something like Ururun Wolf.

Bahamut's slow movement speed is usually used to the player's advantage. Paired with his sluggish attack rate, he has very little pushing power on his own. In an old strategy known as Bahamut stacking, Bahamut's lack of pushing power is taken advantage of to stack him on weak peons before the boss wave of the stage that occur on base hit ([[Dark Souls (Insane)|Dark Souls]] and [[Absolute Defense (Insane)|Absolute Defence]] are great examples of stages this works on). However, his low movement speed also makes him unresponsive to the battle, and in longer stages he will fall behind other faster units.

==='''True Form'''===
Bahamut Cat's True Form takes the purpose of its first two forms and completely flips it on its head. He gains reduced recharge time, greatly increased attack speed (giving him a jaw-dropping 30k DPS at level 30) and movement speed, twice as many knockbacks, and multi-hit attack. His very short foreswing pretty much guarantees at least his first hit (which has most of his damage) if he reaches his target. However, this comes at the cost of his range being cut by more than half, changing him into a suicidal melee unit. Despite the obvious drawbacks, Bahamut can be put to great use against durable enemies with slow attack rates or low range, such as Bore and [[Sunfish Jones]], provided if you support him sufficiently. With proper timing, he can easily infiltrate blindspots of [[:Category:Long Distance Enemies|Long Distance Enemies]] such as [[Mr. Mole]] and [[Henry]] to cripple them, which is part of the main strategy for [[Learned to Love]] and [[False Resurrection]]. Awakened Bahamut is especially good for [[Cats of the Cosmos]], since his True Form is accessible right after beating [[Into the Future]] Chapter 3. His first hit can easily crush most enemy [[Barrier]]s as he follows up with even more attacks.

Timing is absolutely essential to playing Awakened Bahamut due to his above average cost for his role. 3-4 hits off the main target are the minimum for Awakened Bahamut to be worth his cost, otherwise it would be far more cost efficient to simply use cheaper rushers like [[Express Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Bullet Train Cat]] or [[Sanada Yukimura (Uber Rare Cat)|Sanada Yukimura]]. This is most evident in levels like [[:Category:Advent Stages|Advent Stages]], where your goal in certain stages is to maximize his value: like chaining the [[R.Ost]] present in [[Realm of Carnage (Deadly)|Realm of Carnage]], and killing [[Angelic Sleipnir]] and [[Cruel Angel Clionel]] in [[No Plan A (Deadly)|No Plan A]].

To obtain the most value out of Awakened Bahamut, one must know how to perform Bahamut Chaining, where Bahamut juggles enemies by damaging them sufficiently with his first hit to the point where they stagger. As soon as his backswing is done, he rushes up to hit them again and the cycle repeats until either of them is destroyed, potentially allowing him to prevail even at a range disadvantage. This is best done with the likes of [[Rain D.]] due to their numerous knockbacks. Support may be needed to pin these enemies in place so they don't get to Bahamut first.

On a much lesser note, while it is nearly impossible to master, Awakened Bahamut can be used to clip through enemies and cheese a stage or at least do significant damage with the help of a [[Sniper the Cat]] or the normal [[Cat Cannon]] thanks to his high speed. This exploits the fact units do not have a hitbox when they get displaced (which can be caused by the Knockback ability, when they take too much damage or when hit by the default Cat Cannon/Sniper the Cat, as well as the Holy Blast Cannon when against Zombies), where he can slip through unharmed. Awakened Bahamut is also easier to be used than some other rushers, as his backswing has a proper animation for reference, making it much easier to tell when he will start to attack again. This allows you to better gauge the timing of other units, and the Cat Cannon. The [[Ototo Corps#Thunderbolt|Thunderbolt Cannon]] can be used to provide him with openings if you find timing too difficult, and also makes him a viable against enemies with fast attack cycles he cannot hope to reach on his own, such as [[Professor A.]] in 4-crown restrictions.

==='''Talents'''===
Despite not gaining any Stat Talents, whatever Bahamut has are extremely powerful and make him by far one of the top units in the game.

'''[[Explosion]]''' is the star of the show, and definitely the top priority, with a 100% chance to activate when fully upgraded. Because Explosions have consistent spawn areas and the center blast carries the same damage as his first hit, this talent effectively doubles Bahamut's already high DPS and brings it to insane levels against vulnerable enemies, reaching 60K at level 30 (and 80K at level 50)–a number only surpassed by a strengthened [[Lesser Demon Cat (Uber Rare Cat)|Greater Balrog Cat]], yet Bahamut has the advantage of delivering it in a fairly sizeable AoE. His Explosion gives him forward pierce ever so slightly greater than that of [[Red Riding Mina (Special Cat)|Awakened Mina]], except with several times her DPS. Given a stable stepping stone, this allows Bahamut to melt through hard-to-reach backliners such as Angelic Sleipnir and Master A. Due to the lingering effect of Explosions, enemies that get knocked back from the first blast may also get hit by the third blast, allowing him to perform chaining and juggle high knockback enemies like [[Rain D.]] more efficiently, as well as dealing extra damage. This pierce is a quality seen on few other rushers, allowing Bahamut to stand out from others as a unique rusher, as if his godlike damage and DPS isn't enough. As a whole, the Explosion Talent massively increases Awakened Bahamut's damage, and to an extent, sustainability, allowing him to stay in the battle for much longer to deal even more damage. His pierce is capable of sniping peons, completely preventing them from interfering in a chain. While it costs a hefty 235 NP, the value of the investment far outweighs its cost.

It is also worth mentioning the various synergies that Awakened Bahamut has with both uber and non-uber units. With his humongous DPS and pierce, Bahamut can quickly disrupt the enemy frontline, preventing tanks from facetanking too much damage and protecting snipers if enemies get too close. He also works well with other rushers like [[Idi:N (Special Cat)|Idi:NX]] and Yukimura to burst down trait specific threats faster (most evident in [[Birth of True Man]]). Bahamut has notable synergy with Conjurers like [[Master of Mind Soractes (Special Cat)|Master of Mind Soractes]], where the various status effects and extra damage from the Conjure all help keep Bahamut alive longer, allowing him to deal even more damage to the enemy frontline.

'''Movement Speed Up''' is the next most valuable talent, though it is far from being as important as Explosion. An increase in Bahamut's movement speed makes him incredibly aggressive, making infiltrating blindspots and timing him to dodge enemy attacks far easier than before, allowing him to gain more value and justify his higher cost as compared to other rushers. It also allows him to reach the frontline faster, making him more responsive (though it also means you must time other rushers a bit earlier to protect him). It also can allow him to clip through enemies while they are knocked back easier, but this is less important than the other reasons to get this talent.

'''[[Immune to Slow|Slow Immunity]]''' is the least important talent, since Bahamut doesn't live long enough for it to matter much, but it is a nice addition to prevent him from getting slowlocked by weaker Slow enemies like [[Croakley]] and wasting your money. It is also a good quality of life against certain enemies with annoying Slow abilities like Professor A. If you have the NP to afford it, this talent is a neat little bonus, but invest in his other Talents first.

==='''Orb'''===
Bahamut is perhaps the best user of Attack [[Talent Orbs]] in the game. Each orb grade increases damage dealt from the main hit and all hits from Explosion by 5,000, going up to 25,000 extra damage at S-Rank. This greatly increases Bahamut's damage output, especially with the outer blasts of Explosion, going from 44K damage to 69K damage. SoL/UL buff orb also works wonders depending on which part of the game you are in.

Since Awakened Bahamut is a generalist that focuses on damage, a Berserker Orb is the best Orb for him (allowing him to Strengthen by 5%-20% after killing 10 enemies depending on Orb level). With this Orb, you can send Bahamut to kill peons and activate his Strengthen, after which he can proceed to deal increased damage to the main threat. Resist Surge/Wave/Explosion also works well on him to prevent him from rushing into them and dying before reaching his target.
